docs/AUDIT-ACTIONS.md	| `session.revoke` | An admin/security admin revokes OIDC sessions, per-user API tokens and registered SSH keys (`POST /sessions/revoke`). Target is the supplied `sub`, or `*` for all. With `sessions_only` only a session-only cut is stamped: browser sessions end, API tokens and SSH keys keep working. A failure after the session cutoff records completed counts; token and SSH-key operations are both attempted. Counts describe this call only, so a retry counts the remainder. Already-open SSH channels require normal teardown. A person's own sign-out (`POST /auth/logout`) writes the same session-only cut and row, with the person as actor and target, only when the request carried their live session. An identity-provider back- or front-channel logout writes it too, with `source` `idp_back_channel_logout` or `idp_front_channel_logout` | `scope` (`sub` or `all`), `sub` (sub-scoped only), `tokens_revoked`, `ssh_keys_deleted`, `sessions_only` (`true` when the request asked for the session-only cut; both counts are then `0`), `cli_sessions_ended` (CLI sessions this call's cut ended, every scope and source, not those a concurrent cut ended first), `source` (`idp_back_channel_logout` or `idp_front_channel_logout` only); `queued_at` and `token_sha256` (a back-channel logout that was held while the identity provider was unreachable: when it was received, and the SHA-256 of its token, the same value as its `session.revoke.hold` row); `error` on failure | [`internal/api/sessions.go#Server.handleRevokeSessions`](../internal/api/sessions.go); sign-out at [`internal/api/sessions.go#Server.handleLogout`](../internal/api/sessions.go); IdP logout at [`internal/api/signin_denied.go#Server.auditIdPLogoutCtx`](../internal/api/signin_denied.go) | internal | — |	both sides edited the session.revoke row; the merge keeps rc.2's row (queued_at, token_sha256, auditIdPLogoutCtx) with 0.10.5's CLI sign-ins wording
